WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.640
R. Ellis Frazier, Director, Producer, CEO&nbsp;
at BADHOUSE Studios: Three weeks later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.400
we're going to be on some red carpet somewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.440
and William Morris is going to call&nbsp;
someone to sign us. None of that happened.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.800
Film Courage: You've mentioned that a&nbsp;
beautiful lesson for creative people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:18.480
is getting knocked down. Why is that? It&nbsp;
sounds troubling. Why would that be good?

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:29.840
Frazier: It's been said a lot you don't know who&nbsp;
you are until you've been knocked down and figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:39.120
out who you are. Who you are is always in how you&nbsp;
get up and how gracefully you can get up without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:48.320
retaining too much anger and resentment for&nbsp;
things. You're going to get knocked down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:51.760
in the film business. I don't care&nbsp;
if you're how high up you are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:01:06.800
there's going to issues. When you've been&nbsp;
down, then you can get up. We had a film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.600
that we were going to do that was going to be&nbsp;
this bigger film. It was going to be the one that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:21.680
goes up and up. It got all the way to location&nbsp;
scouting everything we had production offices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:26.960
everything and something happened and the&nbsp;
thing just the bottom just dropped out of it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:36.080
and we literally were in the production&nbsp;
office like they said movies off we're like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:43.360
what are we going to do now and a lot of people&nbsp;
just went their separate ways and everything else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:48.720
and and I still remember how&nbsp;
kind of low that felt that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.720
and then it wasn't like you try to save it oh&nbsp;
maybe we can go over here and somebody will do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:04.240
it and that didn't work so we just had nothing we&nbsp;
had to start over from scratch and I thought wow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:13.200
what a great lesson that is what a great humbling&nbsp;
experience so I know now that it's not just going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:20.160
to go just like I want it so we need to be able to&nbsp;
figure out how to bring yourself back up from that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:27.920
and in that is where you learn the most&nbsp;
valuable lessons in life and in film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:36.880
in life obviously it's how you test your your&nbsp;
inner strength and ability to stay focused in film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:42.320
you learn how to do things that you were going to&nbsp;
hire three guys to do now you can hire one guy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:48.240
do and you learn that you can do it with the one&nbsp;
guy if you help you learn how these things all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:55.600
work and how they all kind of work together&nbsp;
and you become a better filmmaker that way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:05.360
so when when you get knocked down that's where the&nbsp;
lessons are I mean like I had said before the the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:10.480
first film we just were kind of on cloud night we&nbsp;
didn't know what was going on we just knew well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.720
three weeks later we're going to be on some&nbsp;
red carpet somewhere and William Morris is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:24.160
going to call someone to sign us none of that&nbsp;
happened so the real lessons were learned after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:33.440
we had that thing fought from under us and&nbsp;
say okay now we can rebuild now we can rebuild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:38.720
with something solid where we know what we're&nbsp;
doing when we know how to get it done and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:44.058
can then look at a investor or look at a&nbsp;
distributor or someone who's giving an mg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.058 --> 00:03:48.800
[a minimum guarantee] or whatever and say I know&nbsp;
exactly how to get this film done we don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:53.760
to depend on anybody that we don't know we know&nbsp;
exactly how to get it done and that confidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:02.160
in that gleam in your eye that confidence&nbsp;
is something that financiers want to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:07.280
that you're not thinking about how great you're&nbsp;
going to be that you're thinking about the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.680
you're thinking about their&nbsp;
investment you're thinking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:18.400
all the elements and that if something happens&nbsp;
you can fix it you're not just going to fall apart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:23.760
on the ground how to pick yourself back&nbsp;
up and pick your production back up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:31.040
and keep it moving forward that's a much better&nbsp;
investment than if you've never been knocked down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.400
can you tell when you go to hire someone&nbsp;
or you have an interview with someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:41.760
for whatever it is whether it's a script&nbsp;
reading a producer and there's something in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:47.360
you that knows that person hasn't been knocked&nbsp;
down and maybe they're not going to weather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.880
a tougher or shoot a little something with a&nbsp;
little more challenge a little more edge to it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:58.560
absolutely you can tell you can it's you can tell&nbsp;
it right in their eyes I mean you don't want to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:07.440
prejudiced against them meaning in that way but&nbsp;
it's a lot of times you can tell by what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:13.600
ask for if they say I need this I need that and&nbsp;
I need this and I and then you say well we might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:16.800
have that with well how are we going&nbsp;
to do the film if we don't have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:24.160
well if you if you haven't done the film without&nbsp;
that then you might be on the wrong film because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:29.200
we may have that and we may be able&nbsp;
to get that but it can't be like well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:40.800
I you have you're doing DP stuff and the guy says&nbsp;
well I can't work without a focus puller well most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:46.960
DPs don't want to work without a focus puller and&nbsp;
to have a focus point we have focus pullers but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:53.520
the DPs that are doing indie films most of them&nbsp;
know that they've at some point had to pull their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:59.680
own focus okay so if you get somebody that says&nbsp;
well I've never worked without a focus puller well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:05.840
that may not be the right guy for us because&nbsp;
even though we'll give him a focus puller we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:15.280
we still it's still potentially problematic that&nbsp;
they have maybe never been on enough films and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:20.800
had those kind of experience so if something&nbsp;
happens that for focus puller get sick or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:27.680
runs off or something they can we can still get&nbsp;
the film done and things don't stop unless things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:33.520
go exactly the way they're supposed to go so I&nbsp;
think that's a decent example of it but but it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:41.760
can be a lot of things it's very rare to find&nbsp;
an actor that has not been knocked down that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:47.840
that's very common they've all had any actor&nbsp;
has dealt with massive amounts of rejection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:55.200
massive amounts of disappointment they know they&nbsp;
were perfect for the role they did a perfect read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:59.200
and they never got the call back or they&nbsp;
got the call back and then they gave the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:05.520
part to the producer's girlfriend or something&nbsp;
like that because they're they they come in pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:13.360
strong in that area of things not going&nbsp;
their way so I give them that credit to and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:19.360
certainly directors and filmmakers you&nbsp;
go through it constantly of of having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:25.520
this is going to go and it doesn't go and&nbsp;
this is going to go and it's going to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:31.440
it's one of the reasons we moved to Mexico we&nbsp;
just just stay stay with them with the real stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:39.200
so based on what someone asks questions and their&nbsp;
needs you can tell how it'll work out I think so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:46.640
if you ask the right questions I asked&nbsp;
what was the lowest budget movie you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:52.400
ever made let's say I’m worth talking a crew&nbsp;
member say have you ever have you done an in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:57.840
if you did student type films things like&nbsp;
that and that'll kind of inform you as to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:07.680
how cooperative they'll be and you know what do&nbsp;
you do if there's not enough time or daylight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:14.800
what do you do and well we'll just shoot&nbsp;
tomorrow no you can't shoot tomorrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:19.360
what are you going to do well they can always&nbsp;
figure something out put the light up here like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:24.560
this we'll do like this we'll bring it in close&nbsp;
we'll just we'll do the reverse so we see the sun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:28.160
and then we'll turn around and light it this&nbsp;
way there's all kinds of things you can do so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:37.200
you're always looking for flexibility you're&nbsp;
looking for people that that feel like they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:42.800
can get it done regardless of the situation in&nbsp;
the scenario it's the most important thing so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:51.440
and I don't see a lot of people that are are&nbsp;
really I've had to say what I can't have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:58.000
person it's not a lot most people in film have&nbsp;
come up through the same system of with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:04.080
friends and in student films and short films&nbsp;
and they've seen it all most of the time so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:09.840
it's not like people coming out of some&nbsp;
prestigious school where they've never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:15.680
seen anything I mean you you see people a lot&nbsp;
of people go to film school and to USC or UCLA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:26.320
and that's great but everybody knows it it's on&nbsp;
set experience is what everybody's looking for.

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:28.597
What lessons have you learned&nbsp;
from getting knocked down?

